N's CEO, Mark Thompson, thanked her for 43 years of great work. Amanpour said she will keep making her podcast, The Ex Files. She also wants to try new projects in the future."], "paragraphs_zh": ["阿曼普说，她5月会停掉自己的节目，6月底正式离开CNN。她在PBS还有一个节目，今年晚些时候也会结束。她说这个决定她想了好几个月。", "阿曼普今年68岁。从2021年起，她一直公开自己治疗卵巢癌的情况。她刚进CNN时只是助理，后来报道了伊拉克、波黑、卢旺达、阿富汗、乌克兰和伊朗等地的大战。", "CNN现在被大卫·埃里森掌管的一家更大的媒体公司收购了。一些CNN记者对此感到担心。阿曼普说，她离开跟新老板没有关系。她说自己会一直支持独立新闻。", "CNN首席执行官马克·汤普森感谢她43年来的出色工作。阿曼普说，她会继续做播客《The Ex Files》，以后也想尝试新项目。"], "body": "Amanpour said she will stop her show in May. She will leave CNN at the end of June. She also has a show on PBS, and that one will end later this year. She said she thought about the decision for many months.\n\nAmanpour is 68 years old. She has been open about her treatment for ovarian cancer since 2021. She started at CNN as an assistant. Later, she reported on big wars in Iraq, Bosnia, Rwanda, Afghanistan, Ukraine and Iran.\n\nCNN is now owned by a bigger media company run by David Ellison. Some CNN journalists are worried about this. Amanpour said her exit is not about the new owners. She said she will always support independent journalism.\n\nCNN's CEO, Mark Thompson, thanked her for 43 years of great work. Amanpour said she will keep making her podcast, The Ex Files. She also wants to try new projects in the future.", "body_zh": "阿曼普说，她5月会停掉自己的节目，6月底正式离开CNN。她在PBS还有一个节目，今年晚些时候也会结束。她说这个决定她想了好几个月。\n\n阿曼普今年68岁。从2021年起，她一直公开自己治疗卵巢癌的情况。她刚进CNN时只是助理，后来报道了伊拉克、波黑、卢旺达、阿富汗、乌克兰和伊朗等地的大战。\n\nCNN现在被大卫·埃里森掌管的一家更大的媒体公司收购了。一些CNN记者对此感到担心。阿曼普说，她离开跟新老板没有关系。她说自己会一直支持独立新闻。\n\nCNN首席执行官马克·汤普森感谢她43年来的出色工作。阿曼普说，她会继续做播客《The Ex Files》，以后也想尝试新项目。", "text": "Amanpour said she will stop her show in May. She will leave CNN at the end of June. She also has a show on PBS, and that one will end later this year. She said she thought about the decision for many months. Amanpour is 68 years old. She has been open about her treatment for ovarian cancer since 2021. She started at CNN as an assistant. Later, she reported on big wars in Iraq, Bosnia, Rwanda, Afghanistan, Ukraine and Iran. CNN is now owned by a bigger media company run by David Ellison. Some CNN journalists are worried about this. Amanpour said her exit is not about the new owners. She said she will always support independent journalism. CNN's CEO, Mark Thompson, thanked her for 43 years of great work. Amanpour said she will keep making her podcast, The Ex Files. She also wants to try new projects in the future.", "vocab": [{"word": "journalist", "pos": "noun", "zh": "记者", "note": "指从事新闻报道的人", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "leave", "pos": "verb", "zh": "离开", "note": "过去式 left，常用 leave a job", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "show", "pos": "noun", "zh": "节目", "note": "电视或广播节目", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "decision", "pos": "noun", "zh": "决定", "note": "make a decision 做决定", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "assistant", "pos": "noun", "zh": "助理", "note": "帮别人做事的人", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "report", "pos": "verb", "zh": "报道", "note": "report on 报道某事", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "owner", "pos": "noun", "zh": "所有者；老板", "note": "拥有公司或东西的人", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "independent", "pos": "adj", "zh": "独立的", "note": "不受别人控制", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}], "title_original": "Christiane Amanpour announces she’s leaving CNN after 43 years", "summary_original": "Anchor’s departure not thought to be linked to company’s recent acquisition by David Ellison’s Skydance Media\nChristiane Amanpour, one of CNN’s most decorated and venerated journalists, is leaving the network after 43 years, she said in a statement on Monday.\nAmanpour, who began working for the then-fledging network as an assistant on the international assignment desk in 1983 and is now its chief international anchor, will end her show Amanpour in May and leave the network at the end of June. She also hosts a show for PBS, Amanpour and Company, that will conclude at the end of this year.\nContinue reading...", "body_original": "Christiane Amanpour announces she’s leaving CNN after 43 years\nAnchor’s departure not thought to be linked to company’s recent acquisition by David Ellison’s Skydance Media\nChristiane Amanpour, one of CNN’s most decorated and venerated journalists, is leaving the network after 43 years, she said in a statement on Monday.\nAmanpour, who began working for the then-fledging network as an assistant on the international assignment desk in 1983 and is now its chief international anchor, will end her show Amanpour in May and leave the network at the end of June. She also hosts a show for PBS, Amanpour and Company, that will conclude at the end of this year.\nIn the statement, Amanpour said she came to the decision to “separate” from the network “after many months of thought and a few sleepless nights”.\n“From my very first big exciting assignment covering the first Gulf war, to the horrors of the Bosnian war, Rwanda, Afghanistan, Ukraine and Iran and all the points in between, it’s the teams and the camaraderie that counted so much, as we together battled to seek the facts, the evidence and the humanity, so that CNN could always broadcast the truth about the world and to the world, without fear nor favor,” she wrote.\nShe ended her statement by encouraging her CNN colleagues: “Remember: always be truthful, not neutral.”\nAmanpour, 68, has been open about her ongoing treatment for ovarian cancer, which she was diagnosed with in 2021.\nHer decision comes at a notable time for the network, which is now part of a larger media conglomerate called Skydance that is run by David Ellison, with heavy backing from Larry Ellison, his Trump-donor father and Oracle co-founder.\nHer exit is not thought to be specifically tied to the network’s new ownership, which is viewed skeptically by some CNN journalists because of its perceived closeness to the Trump administration. But, in her statement, Amanpour seemed to nod to concerns about the network’s future, saying that she “will always be rooting for CNN and maintaining its history of independent journalism”.\nShe expanded on that commitment to independence in a taped message to viewers of her CNN International show on Monday afternoon.\n“You know that I’ve always been fiercely independent, and I will continue to be that for you for as long as I am here and beyond,” she said. “That’s especially important today as we face unprecedented pressures from all sides – political, financial, and also from the rising dangers in the field.”\nIn his own memo, CNN’s CEO, Mark Thompson,\nwho announced earlier this month\nthat he will be remaining with the merged company, thanked Amanpour “for 43 extraordinary years of remarkable journalism, loyalty and friendship”.\n“Christiane has thought carefully about this decision, and she and I have been discussing it for some months,” Thompson wrote. “Like so many CNNers who will hear the news today, I’m very sorry to see her go, but I’ve come to accept that this is the right time for her, both personally and professionally.”\nAmanpour said she will continue hosting her podcast, The Ex Files and that she “[looks] forward to interesting new projects down the line”.\nExplore more on these topics\nCNN\nThe news on TV\nTV news\nTelevision\nTelevision industry\nnews\nShare\nReuse this content", "fetched_at": "2026-10-22T00:07:12.314662", "timeline": [{"i": 0, "w": "Amanpour", "s": 0.3, "e": 0.8, "sm": 300, "em": 800, "cs": 0, "ce": 8, "si": 0}, {"i": 1, "w": "said", "s": 0.8, "e": 1.025, "sm": 800, "em": 1024, "cs": 9, "ce": 13, "si": 0}, {"i": 2, "w": "she", "s": 1.025, "e": 1.162, "sm": 1025, "em": 1162, "cs": 14, "ce": 17, "si": 0}, {"i": 3, "w": "will", "s": 1.163, "e": 1.275, "sm": 1162, "em": 1274, "cs": 18, "ce": 22, "si": 0}, {"i": 4, "w": "stop", "s": 1.275, "e": 1.537, "sm": 1275, "em": 1537, "cs": 23, "ce": 27, "si": 0}, {"i": 5, "w": "her", "s": 1.538, "e": 1.687, "sm": 1537, "em": 1687, "cs": 28, "ce": 31, "si": 0}, {"i": 6, "w": "show", "s": 1